  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,7x-2y=10 \\\\ -2x+y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as \n$x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$",
  "worked_solution": "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7x-2y=10 \\\\ -2x+y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}10 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$",
